File Index

/landing-page.conf
Purpose: New 2026-08-09, replaces the never-wired shatteredarchive-dev-stub.conf (deleted — this is its real successor, not a sibling). Serves the shatteredarchive.dev landing page + /privacy as static files (root /var/www/shatteredarchive-dev, four files bind-mounted individually in each compose file's nginx service), mirroring the routing production's separate host-level nginx does (DSL/nginx/shattered_archive.site's apex block): inline ads.txt/robots.txt, a real sitemap.xml with `=404` (not the SPA fallback), an exact-match /privacy before the catch-all (without it, a missing /privacy silently 200s as the homepage instead of 404ing), and `location /` falling back to index.html. Discovered live: the root-domain server blocks in BOTH edge-subdomains.conf and tls-dev.conf were pure placeholders (`return 200 "ShatteredArchive edge is running."`) that answered every path identically, including /privacy — a real, long-standing gap, not a regression. Included from both schemes alongside the env-switched ad/analytics CSP profile (see security-headers-ads.conf's entry above).

/robots-disallow-all.conf
Purpose: Shared `location = /robots.txt` returning `User-agent: * / Disallow: /` (new 2026-08-05), included by every .dev app/API server block in BOTH schemes (edge-subdomains.conf's :80 blocks and tls-dev.conf's :443 mirrors) — game-client, web-client, game-server, web-server, auth, build, kingdom-tactics, and dsl-log-viewer on the .com side. A location block inside an include works because nginx includes are textual. NOT redundant with "just let it 404": these blocks all end in `location /` proxying to a container whose own conf does `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html`, so /robots.txt would return the app's HTML with a 200 and a crawler would read that as "no rules, crawl everything"; the same SPA fallback also makes every invented path a 200 HTML page, i.e. unbounded crawl waste. Declares no add_header on purpose — one would discard the server block's inherited security headers (nginx does not merge add_header across levels); default_type is safe. Deliberately blocks Mediapartners-Google too since none of these hosts has an ad unit — NEVER point an ad-bearing host here, because blocking that agent is what makes AdSense stop serving. ADVISORY ONLY: nothing is protected by this file (the hub's session guards and the builder's bearer gate do that); it only keeps pages out of search results. The apex shatteredarchive.dev is the one crawlable host and gets its robots.txt inline from the front proxy instead.

/robots-scrum-poker.conf
Purpose: scrum-poker's own `location = /robots.txt` (new 2026-08-05) — the single app host that is both public content and ad-bearing, so it cannot take the blanket robots-disallow-all.conf. Two groups: `Mediapartners-Google: Allow: /` (the AdSense crawler, allowed EVERYTHING including /room/ because AdSlot renders on every route and Google fetches the page an ad sits on to pick a creative — blocking this agent stops ads serving outright per Google's docs, and it leaks nothing since it only fetches URLs an ad request already named, never indexes them, and the room page is client-rendered so the HTML carries no estimates or names); and `*` with `Disallow: /room/` + `Allow: /`, because a room id IS the membership credential and an indexed room URL would hand access to anyone who searched. Longest-match makes the two directives coexist correctly (/room/x: Disallow 7 chars beats Allow 1). ADVISORY ONLY — what actually keeps a room private is the UUID being unguessable. Included in both schemes.

/security-headers-common.conf
Purpose: The three non-CSP baseline headers (nosniff, Referrer-Policy, X-Frame-Options) shared by every browser-facing block, split out 2026-07-31 so a host can take a different CSP without duplicating them. Sets NO Content-Security-Policy on purpose: nginx APPENDS add_header rather than replacing it, so two CSP headers make the browser enforce their INTERSECTION and a "relaxed" profile stacked on the strict one relaxes nothing. Never included directly by a server block — include exactly one profile (security-headers.conf / -scripting.conf / -api.conf) and it pulls this in.

/security-headers-api.conf
Purpose: The same baseline for API-ONLY hosts (JSON/websockets, never a rendered document), with CSP default-src 'none' — with no document to protect, the strictest policy costs nothing and still matters if a JSON endpoint is ever coaxed into rendering as HTML. Also used to restore headers inside locations that declare their own add_header (see the trap note in security-headers.conf).

/security-headers.conf
Purpose: The STRICT CSP profile — the default for BROWSER-FACING server blocks; include once per server{} (it pulls in security-headers-common.conf for the non-CSP headers). script-src 'self': no app on this profile calls the Function constructor and Vite emits external module scripts, which is what makes an injected script inert, so keep 'unsafe-inline'/'unsafe-eval' out. CORRECTED 2026-08-05 — the file's own comment used to ALSO claim no app on the profile contains an inline <script>, which was false when written: apps/scrum-poker-client/index.html has an inline theme-flash guard that this policy has been silently refusing all along (cosmetic only — the app re-reads the same key on mount — which is why nobody noticed). Do not resolve it by adding 'unsafe-inline' here; that weakens every app on the profile for one flash. scrum-poker now takes security-headers-ads.conf in prod, which permits inline for unrelated AdSense reasons and incidentally lets the guard run, so the symptom is currently visible only on the experimental stack. so keep 'unsafe-inline'/'unsafe-eval' out of it; a host that genuinely needs eval takes security-headers-scripting.conf instead, which keeps the exception scoped to one named host rather than loosening this for everyone. style-src DOES need 'unsafe-inline' because React renders style= attributes, which CSP counts as inline styles; that is style injection, not script execution, so it does not weaken the above. connect-src names the auth hub explicitly because every app (including the .com ones) talks to it cross-origin for device credentials and SSO, plus wss://$host to cover the real-time apps without listing each. THE TRAP: nginx add_header does NOT merge across levels — any location declaring its own add_header silently discards every inherited one, so such locations (/data/, /healthz) must include this file AGAIN. Grep for add_header inside locations before assuming coverage.

/security-headers-ads.conf
Purpose: The THIRD-PARTY MONETISATION CSP profile (new 2026-08-05) — the strict profile plus the Google AdSense/GA4/Funding-Choices origins allowlisted across script/frame/img/connect, and NOTHING else. Used by exactly ONE host, scrum-poker.shatteredarchive.dev, selected there via ${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE} so a build with empty ad args keeps the strict profile (prod compose sets this file; experimental keeps the strict one — ads are off on the dev replica, deliberately, because live units served from a dev machine are AdSense invalid traffic). CRITICALLY: script-src does NOT contain 'unsafe-inline'. The first version of this file did, on the assumption AdSense required it; it does not — the loader is fetched from an allowlisted HOST (a host-source match, not an inline one) and creatives render in CROSS-ORIGIN iframes where the parent's script-src does not apply (those are governed by frame-src). Our own init code is external/bundled for the same reason. That reversal is the whole point of the file: 'unsafe-inline' does not create an XSS, it removes the mitigation that would neuter one, and this origin's localStorage holds real per-room credentials (participant secrets, host tokens — see the client's storage.ts). If an ad unit ever blanks with a script-src violation, the fix is a nonce + 'strict-dynamic' (nginx $request_id + sub_filter), NOT blanket 'unsafe-inline' — and note 'strict-dynamic' makes host-source expressions be IGNORED, so it is a rewrite of the policy rather than an addition. Still the only host on this profile because it alone admits third-party script/frames at all: no accounts, no password field, no __Host-sa_session cookie (host-only + HttpOnly, so neither sent to nor readable from this origin), no service key, never calls auth-server. NEVER include on auth (login/signup/reset forms, session cookie) or game-client (already has 'unsafe-eval'; eval + third-party script stacked is a different risk class). Other deliberate deviations from the strict profile: connect-src DROPS https://auth.shatteredarchive.dev (scrum-poker never calls the hub, and the two are same-SITE so a SameSite=Lax session cookie WOULD ride along on such a request); adds Permissions-Policy denying the Privacy Sandbox ad APIs (browsing-topics, join-ad-interest-group, run-ad-auction, attribution-reporting) plus device sensors — a Permissions-Policy denial reaches embedded frames, which is what makes it effective against the creative rather than only our own code; adds Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy same-origin-allow-popups (not same-origin — ad click-throughs need to keep their opener). COEP deliberately unset: require-corp would break both Google tags. Kept in step with the equivalent real headers on the shatteredarchive.dev apex block in DSL/nginx/shattered_archive.site and the meta policy in deploy/index.html. 2026-08-09: gained a second consumer, the shatteredarchive.dev root-domain blocks (edge-subdomains.conf + tls-dev.conf), selected there via ${NGINX_CSP_LANDING_FILE} — same on/off-by-default idiom as ${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE}. Production had no docker-served root-domain block at all before that (the apex never reached this edge — DSL/nginx/shattered_archive.site served it directly); this dev-replica has no equivalent front-proxy layer, so its root-domain blocks serve the landing page + /privacy themselves (see landing-page.conf) and needed the same ad/analytics CSP the real apex declares.

/security-headers-analytics.conf
Purpose: The ANALYTICS-ONLY CSP profile (new 2026-08-14) — the strict profile plus ONLY the Google Analytics (GA4) origins allowlisted across script/img/connect, and deliberately nothing from the ad side (no pagead2.googlesyndication.com, no doubleclick, no fundingchoicesmessages, no frame-src at all). A separate file from security-headers-ads.conf on purpose, not that file with the ad tokens trimmed: anyone auditing "which hosts allow third-party script here" should be able to read this file and see only the two GA origin groups, with no ad-network entries to reason past — a host that later grows an ad unit should switch profiles explicitly, not have ad origins silently appended to this one. Used by soulsteel.shatteredarchive.dev, selected via ${NGINX_CSP_SOULSTEEL_FILE} in edge-subdomains.conf (prod defaults here; experimental keeps the strict profile — same live-dev-traffic-pollutes-analytics reasoning as scrum-poker's ad profile). Like security-headers-ads.conf, script-src does NOT contain 'unsafe-inline' — gtag.js is a host-source match, not inline, and this app's init code (analytics.ts) is an external bundled module. Same eligibility bar as security-headers-ads.conf: no accounts, no password field, no service key calling auth-server (true for soulsteel today; the auth-integrated archive plan in .ai-plans/20260813-1912-soulsteel-auth-archive-dashboard.md is unstarted as of this writing — revisit this profile's eligibility once that lands). Never include on auth or game-client, same reasoning as the ads profile gives for itself. Verified with a real `nginx -t` through the entrypoint's envsubst pass.

/security-headers-scripting.conf
Purpose: The RELAXED CSP profile — same as the strict one but with script-src 'unsafe-eval' and worker-src 'self' blob:. Used by exactly one host, game-client.shatteredarchive.dev, which is the only app in the repo that calls the Function constructor or bundles an interpreter. Added 2026-07-31 after the strict rollout hard-broke that app: fengari-web (Lua) evaluates Function("return ()=>void 0;") at MODULE SCOPE, unguarded, and luaRuntime.ts imports it statically — so the EvalError fired before React mounted and the whole client failed to boot, not just Lua. skulpt (Python) and the user-script sandbox itself (userScripts/runtime.ts, globalRuntime.ts) need it too, so no bundler/library swap removes the requirement — running user-authored code IS the feature. Include INSTEAD of security-headers.conf, never alongside it (see the intersection trap in security-headers-common.conf). worker-src covers the optional @huggingface/transformers STT engine's blob: WASM worker; note its model download from huggingface.co is still blocked by connect-src, deliberately unresolved. 2026-07-31: connect-src gained https://shatteredarchive.com — the C# site service the client calls for cloud-sync and /contribute/*, which was missing, so those fetches died as a bare "Failed to fetch" (a CSP refusal carries no status code and never reaches the server log). No longer included directly: the game-client block selects this file or the -dev variant below via ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE}.

/security-headers-scripting-dev.conf
Purpose: Dev-machine variant of security-headers-scripting.conf, selected by ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE} (same idiom as ${NGINX_TLS_INCLUDE_FILE}). Differs on exactly ONE token and must stay that way: connect-src names http://localhost:5000 — where the local stack publishes the C# site service — instead of production's https://shatteredarchive.com. That origin must never reach the production profile, where it would name whatever the VISITOR happens to run on their own :5000.

/tls-dev.conf
Purpose: Configures HTTPS routing and proxying for multiple project services including game client, web client, and API servers. MUST mirror edge-subdomains.conf's HTTP (port 80) blocks route-for-route, one file per scheme — there is no shared/DRY'd routing between the two. Phase F: added kingdom-tactics.shatteredarchive.dev's HTTPS block; a real deploy gap (this block didn't exist for the first deploy pass — https:// 404'd at the default catch-all since kt-server's own KT_CLIENT_ALLOWED_RETURN_ORIGINS is https-only) caught by live-testing the real public hostname end to end, not by review. Any FUTURE new subdomain added to edge-subdomains.conf needs the identical block added HERE too, or it silently only half-works (HTTP fine, HTTPS 404). Scrum Poker (2026-07-28) followed that rule from the start — its HTTPS block landed alongside the HTTP one, and route parity between the two was checked mechanically (both expose exactly /api/scrum/, /ws/scrum, = /health, /). The websocket block matters especially here: the client picks wss:// whenever the page is https, so an HTTPS block missing /ws/scrum would leave a room that loads but never connects. 2026-07-29: the auth + builder edge rate limits were mirrored here the same way — auth gained the new /api/auth/ block and a limit_req on /api/, build gained one on /api/ — because the limit_req_zone declarations live in edge-subdomains.conf (http context, above this include) but the limit_req directives that USE them must be repeated per location in both schemes. Omitting them here would rate-limit HTTP while leaving HTTPS — the scheme everyone actually uses — wide open. The build /api/ client_max_body_size 3m fix (1m built-in default vs the app's 2mb import/snippets routes) is mirrored here for exactly the same reason: HTTPS is the scheme real imports come in over, so fixing only the HTTP block would have left the 413 in place where it actually bites. 2026-07-30: every server block here also includes security-headers.conf (or -api.conf), mirroring edge-subdomains.conf — CSP on the HTTP blocks alone would protect the scheme nobody uses. 2026-07-31, same trap from the other side: the game-client block's CSP was loosened in edge-subdomains.conf ONLY, so the browser (which uses https on this machine) kept enforcing the old policy and the C# site fetches kept failing — this block now includes security-headers-scripting-dev.conf. Note it hardcodes that filename rather than the ${NGINX_CSP_SCRIPTING_FILE} the :80 block uses: envsubst runs over the default.conf.template mount only, never over files in this directory, so a ${...} here would reach nginx literally. Correct anyway — this file IS the dev half of the tls-dev/tls-off pair, so only the dev CSP profile can belong in it. 2026-08-05: the scrum-poker HTTPS block hit that trap for real — it was first written as `include .../${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE};` to mirror the :80 block's new env-switched ad CSP and failed `nginx -t` with `open() ".../${NGINX_CSP_SCRUM_FILE}" failed (2: No such file or directory)`. Now hardcodes security-headers.conf, which is also the correct constant: this file is loaded only by the experimental/local stack, where ads and analytics are deliberately off. This note existed BEFORE that mistake — read it before adding a variable to this directory. 2026-08-09: the root-domain block (was a literal `return 200 "...edge is running..."` placeholder, never actually serving the landing page — a real, long-standing gap, not a regression) now serves the real content via `include landing-page.conf`, same as edge-subdomains.conf's HTTP mirror, and hit the identical ${...} trap: hardcodes security-headers.conf for the same reason the scrum-poker block does. 2026-08-14: the soulsteel block gained the equivalent comment for its own new ${NGINX_CSP_SOULSTEEL_FILE} — hardcodes security-headers.conf here too (this file loads only on the experimental/local stack, which runs analytics off by default), swap to security-headers-analytics.conf plus a TEST VITE_GA_ID to exercise it locally over https.

/tls-off.conf
Purpose: This file serves as a placeholder configuration indicating no local TLS server blocks are included.
